("explain the internet to a medieval peasant",
"""Imagine that every town had a great hall of messengers, and that these messengers could run faster than any horse, faster than lightning, carrying letters between any two halls in the world in less time than it takes to say a prayer. Imagine that every family had a small window in their home, a slate that glows, through which they could send a letter to anyone, read any book ever written, hear any song ever sung, and see the faces of distant friends while speaking to them. That is the internet. It is a vast web of these messengers and windows, and most people use it chiefly to look at pictures of cats and argue with strangers, which I suspect you would find very familiar."""),

