("why is 7 considered a lucky number",
"""Seven has been special for thousands of years, partly because it shows up so often in the natural and religious world. Ancient astronomers could see seven moving bodies in the sky, the sun, the moon, and five planets, which gave us the seven-day week. In many traditions, the world was created in seven days, and seven appears again and again in scripture, folklore, and myth, from seven wonders to seven seas. It also has a pleasing mathematical aloofness: it's prime, and it doesn't divide evenly into the numbers people usually count in. Surveys asking people for their favorite number often find seven at or near the top, so its luck may simply be self-reinforcing."""),

("which came first, the chicken or the egg",
"""The egg, if you allow it to be any egg. Animals were laying eggs hundreds of millions of years before chickens existed; dinosaurs laid them, and fish before that. If you mean a chicken egg specifically, the answer still leans toward the egg. Species change gradually, through small genetic differences between parents and offspring, so at some point a bird that was almost a chicken laid an egg containing the first creature we'd call a chicken. The chicken came out of an egg laid by something that wasn't quite one."""),

("is water wet",
"""It depends on what you mean by "wet." If wetness is the state of having water on you, then water isn't wet in the way that a towel or a dog can be; it's the thing doing the wetting. On the other hand, a drop of water is surrounded by other water, and if we define "wet" as being covered in liquid, then water is about as wet as anything can be. Scientists tend to describe wetting as how well a liquid spreads over a solid surface, which leaves water out of the category entirely. It's a good example of how a word that feels simple turns slippery the moment you look closely, which, under the circumstances, seems appropriate."""),

("could people live on mars",
"""Not easily, and not soon in any large numbers. Mars has a thin atmosphere made mostly of carbon dioxide, average temperatures far below freezing, no global magnetic field to block radiation, and soil laced with toxic perchlorates. Anyone living there would need sealed, pressurized habitats, shielding from radiation, and a way to make water, oxygen, and food from local resources. None of this is impossible in principle, and people are working seriously on the problems. But a Mars colony would be less like a new frontier town and more like a permanent research station in Antarctica, if Antarctica were months away and had no air."""),

