("why do we have pinky toes",
"""Your little toes help with balance more than they get credit for. When you stand, walk, or push off the ground, your toes spread your weight across a wider base, and the outer toes help stabilize the outside edge of your foot. They're much smaller than our ancestors' toes because, as humans became upright walkers rather than climbers, our feet evolved into stiffer platforms for walking long distances, with the big toe doing most of the pushing. The pinky toe is a modest remnant of a grasping foot, still pulling its weight, as you discover painfully whenever it meets a piece of furniture."""),

("what's the longest word in english",
"""It depends on what you're willing to count. The longest word in major dictionaries is usually "pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis," forty-five letters, a lung disease caused by inhaling fine volcanic dust, though it was coined in the 1930s mainly to be the longest word. "Antidisestablishmentarianism," at twenty-eight letters, is the classic example of a word that grew long through ordinary use. If you count technical chemical names, there's no real limit, since some protein names run to tens of thousands of letters, though no one considers those true words."""),

("make up a new holiday",
"""I propose Unfinished Day, held on the first Saturday in November. On Unfinished Day, everyone is encouraged to spend a few hours on a project they abandoned: the half-knitted scarf, the novel stalled at chapter three, the guitar in the closet. There is no pressure to finish anything; the point is simply to visit it again. The traditional greeting is "How far did you get?", and the traditional answer is "Farther than last year." The holiday food is leftovers, naturally."""),

("whats the best way to apologize to my cat",
"""Cats don't understand apologies, but they do understand peace offerings and good manners. Give them space first; a cat who's annoyed with you wants distance, not hugs. Then sit nearby, quietly, and let them come to you. A slow blink, closing your eyes gently and opening them again, is widely interpreted by cat behaviorists as a friendly signal, and many cats will return it. A favorite treat or a few minutes with a feather toy wouldn't hurt either. Within an hour, they'll likely have forgiven you, or at least decided you're useful enough to keep."""),

("why do people like scary movies",
"""Because they let us feel fear without being in danger, and that turns out to be a surprisingly pleasant combination. A frightening scene sets off the body's alarm system: your heart races, your muscles tense, adrenaline floods in. But part of your mind knows you're safe on a sofa, so the experience becomes a thrill rather than a threat, and the relief afterward feels good. Scary movies also let us rehearse fear, imagining how we'd cope with danger, and they bring people together: there's real closeness in grabbing someone's arm in the dark and then laughing at yourself when the cat jumps out of the cupboard."""),

